{"_id":"68a736218ec3d8323af18fd9","nodeId":"qdA6bK9ZkP8p0_NH_wMuj","roadmapSlug":"java","createdAt":"2025-08-21T15:07:13.404Z","description":"# Abstraction\n\nThe abstract keyword in Java is used to declare a class or a method that cannot be instantiated directly or must be implemented by subclasses, respectively. It is a key part of Java's abstraction mechanism, allowing developers to define abstract classes and methods that provide a blueprint for other classes.","resources":[{"type":"article","title":"Java Abstract Classes","url":"https://jenkov.com/tutorials/java/abstract-classes.html","_id":"692485f1467651eddb4d3350"},{"type":"article","title":"Java Interfaces vs. Abstract Classes","url":"https://jenkov.com/tutorials/java/interfaces-vs-abstract-classes.html","_id":"692485f1467651eddb4d3351"}],"updatedAt":"2025-11-24T16:21:05.627Z","lessonPacks":[],"paidResources":[{"_id":"6a5e04defb5fcb94d0416bad","title":"DataCamp - Java Fundamentals","partner":"DataCamp","type":"course","url":"https://datacamp.pxf.io/GbYnP9"}],"contribution":"https://github.com/kamranahmedse/developer-roadmap/tree/master/roadmaps/java/content/qdA6bK9ZkP8p0_NH_wMuj.md"}